  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Atul Bansal vs State Of U.P. And Another on 18 November, 2020 Author: Sanjay Kumar Singh Bench: Sanjay Kumar Singh High Court Of Judicature At Allahabad ?Court No. - 6 Case :- Application U/S 482 No. - 16724 of 2020 Applicant :- Atul Bansal Opposite Party :- State of U.P. and Another Counsel for Applicant :- Amit Kumar Srivastava,Rupesh Kumar Singh",
    "facts": "Brief facts of this case as emerged from the record are that on 20.10.2011 on the direction of District Magistrate, an inspection of the shop/premises of firm Bansal brothers was carried out by Sunil Kumar, authorized officer in presence of R.N. Pandey, S.D.M., Aonla and samples of Besan and Ghee were taken by them. Thereafter, samples were sent for analysis. Food analyst submitted report no. 6595 dated 15.12.2011 regarding seized Besan with the opinion that 'according to regulation number 2.2.2-8, 2.2.2-9 and 2.2.2-10 of Food Safety & Standards (Packaging & Labeling) Regulation 2011, manufacturer has not declared batch No., Mfg date and best before respectively on the empty packet and sample having fungus and non permitted synthetic colour Metanil Yellow which are harmful for human consumption. Hence the sample is misbranded and unsafe'. Another report no. 6589 dated 15.12.2011 was also submitted by Food Analyst regarding seized Ghee with the opinion that 'Butyrorefractometer Reading at 40.C exceeds to prescribed maximum limit of 43.0 for ghee and Richert value is much less than minimum limit of 26.0. Hence, sample is substandard'. Thereafter, required sanctioned for prosecution against the accused persons was also granted on 16.02.2012 by the Commissioner, Food Safety and Drug Administration U.P. Lucknow. Food Safety Officer, Bareilly after completing all the required formalities filed complaint dated 23.02.2012 against applicant Atul Bansal and co-accused Anuj Bansal for violation of Section 26 (2) (i) (ii) (iii), 27 (2) ( c) of Food Safety & Standard Act, 2006 and Regulation no. 2.2.2-8, 2.2.2-9 and 2.2.2-10 of Food Safety and Standard (Packaging & Labeling) Regulation 2011, which are punishable under Section 51, 52, 59(i) and 63 of Food Safety and Standard Act, 2006. On 07.09.2013 charges have been framed against the applicant and co-accused Anuj Bansal. After about three and half years, applicant alongwith co-accused Anuj Bansal jointly moved an application under Section 245(2) Cr.P.C., which was rejected by aforesaid impugned order dated 27.02.2017 and upheld by the revisional court vide order dated 10.01.2020.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the applicant assailing the aforesaid impugned orders submits that both the aforesaid impugned orders have been passed without applying judicial mind. The application under Section 245 (2) Cr.P.C. of the applicant has been illegally rejected in a cryptic manner. Both the courts below have not gone through the materials available on record. Lastly, it is submitted that no prima facie offence under Sections under Sections 26, 27, 51, 52, 59 & 63 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 is made out against the applicant, therefore, impugned orders are liable to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. On the other hand learned AGA submits that in the present case trial has proceeded further. Opposite party no.2/complainant has been examined as PW-1 and Rajeev Agarwal has been examined as PW-2 under Section 246 Cr.P.C. This fact has been mentioned by the applicant in paragraph no. 18 of the application. It is also submitted that it is well settled that disputed question of facts cannot be taken into consideration at the stage of deciding the application under Section 245 Cr.P.C. Lastly, it is submitted that there is no illegality in the aforesaid impugned orders as the same have been passed in the light of well settled principle of law laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court, therefore, application is liable to be dismissed. Here, it it relevant to mention that the Apex Court in case of Palwinder Singh Vs. Balvinder Singh 2009 (3) SCC 850 has held that the jurisdiction of Sessions Judge at the time of discharge is very limited.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the applicant assailing the aforesaid impugned orders submits that both the aforesaid impugned orders have been passed without applying judicial mind. The application under Section 245 (2) Cr.P.C. of the applicant has been illegally rejected in a cryptic manner. Both the courts below have not gone through the materials available on record. Lastly, it is submitted that no prima facie offence under Sections under Sections 26, 27, 51, 52, 59 & 63 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 is made out against the applicant, therefore, impugned orders are liable to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. On the other hand learned AGA submits that in the present case trial has proceeded further. Opposite party no.2/complainant has been examined as PW-1 and Rajeev Agarwal has been examined as PW-2 under Section 246 Cr.P.C. This fact has been mentioned by the applicant in paragraph no. 18 of the application. It is also submitted that it is well settled that disputed question of facts cannot be taken into consideration at the stage of deciding the application under Section 245 Cr.P.C.",
    "respondent_arguments": "Lastly, it is submitted that there is no illegality in the aforesaid impugned orders as the same have been passed in the light of well settled principle of law laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court, therefore, application is liable to be dismissed.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Here, it it relevant to mention that the Apex Court in case of Palwinder Singh Vs. Balvinder Singh 2009 (3) SCC 850 has held that the jurisdiction of Sessions Judge at the time of discharge is very limited."
  },
